In order to liquefy natural gas, the heat in the process of natural gas cooling, condensation and supercooling must be removed, that is, the corresponding cooling capacity is required to exchange with it.
The refrigeration system for natural gas liquefaction is very mature, and the commonly used processes include:
The step refrigeration cycle was first applied to liquefied natural gas products in 1939. It was installed in Cleveland in the United States, using NH3 and C2H4 as the first and second stage refrigerants.
The classical step refrigeration cycle includes several relatively independent and connected cooling stages. Since the refrigerant is generally compressed by multi-stage compressor, in each cooling stage, the refrigerant can evaporate under several pressures and be divided into several temperature levels to cool the natural gas, and the refrigerant evaporated under each pressure enters the corresponding compressor stage for compression.
